  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly quiet through tonight. \n\nHigh pressure remains in control of our weather through tonight. \nOnly expecting some clouds passing by at times. Rather light NW \nto W winds become local seabreezes along the coast by mid to\nlate morning. Near to slightly above normal temperatures\nanticipated. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A round or two of scattered showers and\nthunderstorms possible Thu afternoon into Fri, along with \nsummerlike humidity.\n\nModel guidance was in relatively good agreement on the overall \nsynoptic pattern. However, there are still some timing\ndifferences yet to be resolved. A low pressure should be moving \nacross southeast Canada, pushing its associated fronts past\nsouthern New England. This should generate a round our two of\nshowers or thunderstorms from some time Thursday afternoon into \nFriday. The greatest risk for thunderstorms will be in the warm \nsector ahead of a cold front. A faster timing would result in a \ngreater risk for strong to possibly severe thunderstorms\nThursday afternoon. At this time, still favoring this risk to\nremain mainly to our west. These details could change with later \nforecasts.\n\nEven if the stronger convection remains to our west, noticeably \nhigher humidity should still arrive. Surface dew points should rise \nwell through the 60s into the lower 70s for some.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mainly dry and seasonable weather follows for\nthis weekend. Temperatures may trend above normal early next \nweek.\n\nHigh pressure builds in behind the departing shortwave/cold\nfront for this weekend. The result will be mainly dry and\nseasonable weather Sat into Sun. Ensembles indicating upper\nlevel flow may become more westerly early next week, allowing\nfor temperatures to trend above normal.",
